// design 158 unit 3, freestanding: the dump path itself, on EVERY architecture.
//
// The companion to `taskdump.saw`, which spawns real tasks and is arm64-only
// until DF-158c is fixed. This one runs everywhere and proves the parts that
// have nothing to do with having tasks: the backtrace table is linked into the
// kernel image, the walker compiles and links freestanding under
// `--no-hidden-alloc`, it runs on bare metal, and it reaches the console
// through the same `__saw_rt_write` seam `print` uses.
//
// It also pins the OTHER half of the panic contract: a program with no live
// task takes the runtime seam directly WITH its message, so a panic here prints
// exactly what it printed before design 158 and nothing else. The dump above
// the panic line comes from the explicit call; nothing is appended below it.

import kcore
import std.task.{dump_tasks}

@export("kmain")
func kmain() {
    dump_tasks()
    panic("SOS task dump: no tasks here")
}
